# 1.Declarative Pipeline (most common)
🔹 Syntax: Simplified and structured (uses pipeline { } block)
🔹 File: Usually defined in Jenkinsfile
🔹 Purpose: Recommended for most use cases — easier to read, maintain, and validate.

pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building...'
            }
        }
        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                echo 'Testing...'
            }
        }
        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                echo 'Deploying...'
            }
        }
    }
}

✅ Best for: Teams that want readability, structure, and built-in error handling.

# 2. Scripted Pipeline

🔹 Syntax: Pure Groovy code (more flexible, but complex)
🔹 File: Also defined in a Jenkinsfile
🔹 Purpose: Used for advanced logic and dynamic pipeline behaviors.

node {
    stage('Build') {
        echo 'Building...'
    }
    stage('Test') {
        echo 'Testing...'
    }
    stage('Deploy') {
        echo 'Deploying...'
    }
}

✅ Best for: Complex CI/CD logic, loops, conditionals, and custom Groovy scripting.

# 3. Multibranch Pipeline

🔹 Concept: Automatically creates pipelines for each branch or PR in a source control repo (GitHub, Bitbucket, etc.)
🔹 Each branch has its own Jenkinsfile
🔹 Jenkins automatically discovers and builds branches.

✅ Best for: Repos with multiple branches (e.g., dev, staging, main) that all use CI/CD.

# Bonus Types (Less Common)

Pipeline with Shared Libraries → Reusable pipeline code across multiple projects.

Blue Ocean Pipeline → Visual representation of declarative pipelines (UI plugin).


